<h2>Different foundations</h2>
<p>Infringement vindicates a statutory monopoly. Registration under the Trade Marks Act, 1999 confers, by Section 28, the exclusive right to use the mark in relation to the registered goods and services; Section 29 catalogues the ways that right is invaded — identical marks on identical goods, deceptively similar marks with a likelihood of confusion, and use that takes unfair advantage of or damages a reputed mark even on dissimilar goods. The plaintiff&rsquo;s title is the registration certificate, presumed valid under Section 31.</p>
<p>Passing off vindicates a trading reality. No certificate is needed and none suffices: the claimant must establish the classical trinity — goodwill in the mark or get-up, misrepresentation by the defendant likely to deceive the public into believing its goods or services are the claimant&rsquo;s, and resulting damage. The action polices honesty in trade, which is why it reaches subject matter the register may not: trade dress, packaging, business styles, and marks whose registration is pending or refused.</p>

<h2>The comparison that matters</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Infringement</th><th>Passing off</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Source of right</td><td>Registration (Sections 27(1), 28)</td><td>Goodwill built by use (Section 27(2) preserves the action)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>What plaintiff proves</td><td>Registration + use of an identical/deceptively similar mark within Section 29</td><td>Goodwill + misrepresentation + damage (or its likelihood)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Defendant&rsquo;s added honesty defences</td><td>Statutory defences (Sections 30, 34, 35 — descriptive use, prior user, own name)</td><td>Honest and sufficient differentiation can itself defeat misrepresentation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Forum</td><td>Section 134: suit where the registered proprietor resides or carries on business; commercial courts regime applies</td><td>Ordinary CPC rules via Section 20 — generally the defendant&rsquo;s forum or where the cause of action arises (unless combined with infringement in one suit)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reach</td><td>Limited to registered goods/services, extended for reputed marks by Section 29(4)</td><td>Reaches any field where the goodwill extends and deception is likely</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vulnerability</td><td>Stands or falls with the registration (rectification risk)</td><td>Independent of the register; survives even against registered defendants on prior use</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why suits plead both</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Insurance against rectification.</strong> If the registration is attacked, the passing off claim keeps the suit alive on goodwill and prior use.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Coverage beyond the specification.</strong> Passing off captures the defendant&rsquo;s adjacent goods and services that the registration&rsquo;s class entries miss.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Prior-user conflicts.</strong> Where both sides hold registrations, infringement claims can neutralise each other — Section 28(3) — leaving passing off as the operative battleground of priority and reputation.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Fuller interim narrative.</strong> Actual-confusion evidence, sales and advertising figures assembled for passing off strengthen the discretionary case for an injunction even on the infringement side.</div>
</div>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Plead the two actions separately and completely: the passing off paragraphs must stand on goodwill, misrepresentation and damage even if every registration paragraph were struck out.</p>
  <p>Evidence of use wins both actions: invoices from the earliest years, advertising spend, distributor networks and sales figures should be collected and exhibited systematically, not summarised.</p>
  <p>Defendants should audit the register and the marketplace at once — a rectification petition and a prior-use defence are different levers, and the choice between differentiation and contest is best made before the written statement.</p>
  <p>Remember limitation&rsquo;s practical edge: delay rarely bars a continuing wrong, but it corrodes the interim injunction, which is where most trademark litigation is really decided.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the general framework of the two actions for information; it is not advice on any particular dispute, and the interlocutory outcome in each case turns on its own record of use, registration and market reality.</div>
